What is a Shell Script?
A shell script is a plain text file that contains a series of commands, which are executed by the Unix shell — a command-line interpreter.
It usually includes comments that describe what each part of the script does.
  •	It allows the use of if-else statements and loops.
  •	Executes commands one by one, line by line.
  •	It combines multiple commands and runs them together.
  •	Unlike the terminal where you type one command at a time, a shell script automates the process.
________________________________________
When Do We Need a Shell Script?
  •	When you need to run multiple shell commands, especially if you’ll run them again later.
  •	If you know the steps of a task, you can write them into a script and reuse it.
  •	Shell scripts help automate repetitive tasks.
  •	They reduce the chance of human error.
  •	Scripts can complete tasks faster than humans.
  •	Even for tasks you rarely do — if they’re complex, scripting helps!
________________________________________
Why Use Shell Scripts?
  •	Fast to write and easy to learn.
  •	No need to worry about low-level programming stuff.
  •	Great for quick automation.
  •	Anything you can do in the terminal, you can automate.
  •	Useful for speeding up deployment.
  •	Makes it easy to share or hand over tasks to others.
  •	Acts as documentation for what steps were taken.
  •	Saves time and increases efficiency.

